# Task 7: Gaussian Elimination and LU Decomposition

## 1. Objective

The aim is to solve systems of equations using LU decomposition and compare the result with a direct solution.

## 2. Inputs

The program uses a 3 by 3 matrix and three sets of measurements.

## 3. Mathematical Operations

LU decomposition breaks a matrix into two simpler matrices. The program uses these matrices to solve each set of measurements.

It also calculates the residual to check how close the solution is to the correct answer.

## 4. Original Output

The program calculates a solution for each measurement set. The residuals are zero or very close to zero. The direct solution for the first set is [5, 5, 5].

## 5. Modified Input

I changed the first value in the first measurement set from 15.0 to 17.0.

## 6. Observation

Changing the measurement value changes the calculated solution. The other measurement sets remain unchanged.

## 7. Interpretation

The results show that LU decomposition can solve several systems using the same matrix. The small residual values show that the calculated answers are accurate.

## 8. Related Real-World Problem

This method can be used to estimate temperatures in three connected rooms in a building. The equations describe how heat moves between the rooms.

## 9. Assumptions

The room connections are known, and the same equations apply during each measurement. The heat measurements can change over time.

## 10. New Input Data

The heat measurements are represented by three sets of values. One value is changed to show how the solution responds to a new measurement.

## 11. Output

The program calculates new values for the three unknowns when the measurement data changes.

## 12. Interpretation of the Modified Problem

The results show how changes in heat measurements affect the calculated values. LU decomposition helps solve the equations efficiently when the same matrix is used repeatedly.